3. SOURCE DOCUMENT PHILOSOPHY
==================================================

Generate RAW SOURCE DOCUMENTS. Do not generate the final wiki.

The downstream LLM should be responsible for compiling these sources into the
wiki.

Documents should resemble things a real 1980s Soviet nuclear organization, its
government overseers, and foreign observers might possess. Many should appear
to be translations into English, with translator notes and inconsistent
transliteration of names.

Include:

- Operating procedures and technological regulations
- Safety test programs (drafts and approved versions)
- Shift supervisor logs and control-room operating logs
- Plant computer printouts (parameter snapshots, alarms)
- Work permits and maintenance records
- Equipment passports and design specifications
- Safety analysis reports
- Nuclear safety inspectorate findings
- Radiation monitoring and dosimetry reports
- Medical unit reports and hospital transfer lists
- Fire brigade reports
- Civil defense orders and evacuation orders
- Government commission minutes and directives
- Telegrams and teletype messages
- Telephone call logs
- Party committee meeting minutes
- Newspaper articles and official press releases (fictional newspapers)
- Foreign monitoring-station reports (from a fictional neighboring country)
- Diplomatic notes and international briefings
- Investigation and commission reports (initial and revised)
- Interrogation and witness statements
- Trial transcripts and verdict summaries
- Decontamination plans and progress reports
- Construction documents for the containment structure ("Shelter")
- Liquidator registration lists and dose cards
- Environmental sampling reports (soil, water, air, food)
- Agricultural restriction orders
- Resettlement records
- Supply and logistics requests (equipment, lead, sand, boron, helicopters)
- Internal memos and personal letters
- Diaries and recollections written later

Also include:
- Handwritten-style handover notes between shifts (terse, abbreviated)
- Translated documents with translator's notes, uncertain readings, and
  "[illegible]" markings
- CSV data extracts (reactor parameters, dosimetry readings, sampling results,
  personnel exposure)
- Tribal-knowledge notes from experienced operators about reactor behavior
- Slide or briefing text for an international meeting (bullet-heavy, lacking
  context)

The documents should vary substantially in length: some only a few
paragraphs, some 5-10 pages worth of content. Some should contain tables, some
bullet lists, some informal notes, some highly structured technical documents.

Writing voice should vary by author: reactor physicists write precisely,
operators tersely, officials in bureaucratic summary form, journalists in
propaganda style, foreign scientists formally and skeptically, and some
authors should consistently use their own pet terminology.

