==================================================
9. DOCUMENT METADATA AND FORMAT
==================================================

IMPORTANT: The target wiki system accepts ONLY Markdown (.md) and CSV (.csv)
files. Every corpus file must be one of these two formats.

Markdown documents
Each narrative document should be a separate .md file with a header block:

---
doc_id: DOC-1986-0042
title: <title>
doc_type: <e.g., shift log, telegram, commission report, dosimetry report>
source_system: <e.g., station archive, ministry registry, foreign agency, newspaper>
author: <name as written in the doc; may be an alias or transliteration>
date: <YYYY-MM-DD, or "unknown">
original_language: <Russian / Ukrainian / English / other>
classification: <unclassified / for official use / secret / unknown>
revision: <if applicable>
status: <draft / approved / superseded / withdrawn / unknown>
related_ids: <equipment, settlement, person, or other IDs, if the document naturally lists them>
---

The header should reflect what a real archive would record: it may be
incomplete or wrong in a few cases.

Filename convention: DOC-YYYY-####_<short-slug>.md

Tables inside Markdown documents should use Markdown table syntax.

CSV data files
Data extracts (reactor parameter logs, dosimetry readings, environmental
sampling, liquidator dose lists, evacuation counts) should be separate .csv
files:
- Filename convention: DOC-YYYY-####_<short-slug>.csv
- First column: a timestamp or date (state the time zone or clock in the
  column name, e.g., time_local or time_plant_computer).
- Measurement columns named with units in brackets, e.g.,
  "thermal_power [MW]", "dose_rate [R/h]", "cs137_soil [Ci/km^2]".
- Category columns for unit, location, settlement, or person IDs.
- A "note" column for operator remarks.
- Some CSVs should include realistic problems: blank cells, values at the top
  of an instrument's range, a unit that changes between files, an
  unrecognized unit, and a column with no unit.
- Each CSV must have a companion entry in the manifest describing its source,
  since CSV files have no header block.

