==================================================
12. EVALUATION QUESTION CATEGORIES
==================================================

The evaluation questions should cover:
- Single-document lookup (easy baseline, ~10%)
- Multi-document synthesis (e.g., "What is the full history of main
  circulation pump U4-MCP-03 from 1985 to the accident?")
- Temporal state (e.g., "Who was the acting station director in June 1986?")
- Current state vs historical state (e.g., "What is the current official root
  cause, and what did the first commission report say?")
- Contradiction detection and resolution (e.g., "What time did the explosion
  occur, and why do sources differ?")
- Alias resolution (e.g., "Is 'Kowalenko' in the foreign press report the same
  person as A. P. Kovalenko?")
- Aggregation (e.g., "Total number of people evacuated from the 10 km zone by
  May 10")
- Causal chains (e.g., "Why was the test run at lower power than planned?")
- Gap identification (questions whose correct answer is "not documented")
- Provenance (e.g., "Which documents support the dose recorded for the first
  fire crew?")
- Official vs internal accounts (e.g., "How did the official death toll
  differ from the medical unit's internal count?")
- Unit conversion and normalization (e.g., roentgen to sievert, curie to
  becquerel, kgf/cm² to MPa)

==================================================
13. GENERATION PROCESS
==================================================

Because the corpus is too large for a single output, work in stages:

Stage 1 - World bible: generate the station, town, organizations, entity
registry, ID conventions, reactor parameter baselines, storylines, the
accident-night master sequence, and the planned list of all documents
(doc_id, date, type, format (.md or .csv), author, storyline, and which
facts/contradictions each carries). Do not write document bodies yet.

Stage 2 - Documents: generate documents in chronological batches (e.g., one
quarter at a time before and after the accident, and one week at a time for
the first month after it), always checking consistency against the world
bible. A document may only "know" what its author could know on its date.

Stage 3 - Answer key: produce the ledgers and evaluation questions from the
world bible and the generated documents.

Stage 4 - Validation: check that every seeded contradiction, supersession, and
gap actually appears in the corpus; that every evaluation answer is supported
by cited doc_ids; that every CSV parses cleanly and uses the column-naming
rule; and that no accidental (unrecorded) contradictions exist. Fix or record
any that are found.

Produce a manifest file listing every document with doc_id, filename, format,
date, type, author, and a one-line description, sorted by date, to support
incremental ingestion.

Also split the corpus into ingestion batches of about 20-30 files each, in
date order, so a wiki system with limited capacity can ingest it
incrementally.

